## Method and reasoning
Let P[r][c] sum the rectangle from (1,1) through (r,c). Build it by adding the top and left prefixes and subtracting their common corner. For a query subtract the rows above and columns to the left, then add back their intersection. A zero row and column handle boundary queries. Use 64-bit sums.

## Walk through the sample
For rows 1–2 and columns 2–3, the large prefix totals 17. Removing column 1 subtracts 1 + 4, leaving 12. A zero prefix border makes a query starting in row 1 use the same arithmetic.

## Complexity
Time O(nm+q), space O(nm).

## Reference implementation
~~~
#include <bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;

int main() {
    ios::sync_with_stdio(false);
    cin.tie(nullptr);
    int n, m, q;
    cin >> n >> m >> q;
    vector<vector<long long>> p(n + 1, vector<long long>(m + 1));
    for (int r = 1; r <= n; ++r)
        for (int c = 1; c <= m; ++c) {
            long long x;
            cin >> x;
            p[r][c] = x + p[r - 1][c] + p[r][c - 1] - p[r - 1][c - 1];
        }
    while (q--) {
        int a, b, c, d;
        cin >> a >> b >> c >> d;
        cout << p[c][d] - p[a - 1][d] - p[c][b - 1] + p[a - 1][b - 1] << '\n';
    }
}
